  "text": "umably, the maximum volume of the pyramid with base $\\triangle AI_1I_2$ and apex $O$ occurs when the point $X$ is at the center of the circle formed by the intersection of the sphere and the plane that contains the midpoint of $AB$ and is perpendicular to $AB$. This is because the maximum height of the pyramid would be the radius of the sphere, which is 10 units.\n\nLet's calculate the maximum possible volume of the pyramid with base $\\triangle AI_1I_2$ and apex $O$ as $X$ varies along the circle.\n\n1. **Calculate the area of $\\triangle AI_1I_2$:**\n\nSince $I_1$ and $I_2$ are the incenters of $\\triangle ABX$ and $\\triangle ACX$ respectively, and $X$ is at the center of the circle, the triangle $\\triangle AI_1I_2$ is an equilateral triangle with side length equal to the radius of the sphere, which is 10 units.\n\nThe area of an equilateral triangle with side length $s$ is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2\n\\]\nSubstituting $s = 10$:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 10^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 100 = 25\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\n2. **Calculate the height of the pyramid:**\n\nThe height of the pyramid is the radius of the sphere, which is 10 units.\n\n3. **Calculate the volume of the pyramid:**\n\nThe volume $V$ of a pyramid is given by:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\text{Base Area} \\times \\text{Height}\n\\]\nSubstituting the base area and height:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 25\\sqrt{3} \\times 10 = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 250\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{250\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible volume of the pyramid with base $\\triangle AI_1I_2$ and apex $O$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{250\\sqrt{3}}{3}}\n\\]",
